@page "/help/keyboard"
@attribute [AllowAnonymous]

@* Pure static content: no interactivity, so this stays static SSR (no @rendermode) and renders
   instantly for the `?` shortcut, which opens it in a new tab from anywhere in the app. *@

<PageTitle>Keyboard Shortcuts - Blocwerk</PageTitle>

<div class="keyshort-page">
    <div class="page-header">
        <a href="/" class="back-link">&larr; Home</a>
        <h1>Keyboard Shortcuts</h1>
        <p class="description">
            These are desktop shortcuts &mdash; they need a physical keyboard. While you are typing in a
            text field, shortcuts never fire, so you can name a hold &ldquo;amber&rdquo; without triggering
            the Add, Move, Border and Paint tools. <kbd>Enter</kbd> and <kbd>Space</kbd> also stay with a
            button, link or label that currently has focus, so tabbing to a button and pressing it still
            does exactly what the button says &mdash; even where the page lists another action for
            <kbd>Enter</kbd>.
        </p>
        <p class="description">
            Some surfaces take the keyboard over completely: while a review stepper, an image lightbox or
            a modal dialog is on screen, the shared shortcuts below (including the wall carousel arrows)
            are all inactive and only that surface's own keys apply.
        </p>
    </div>

    <section class="keyshort-section">
        <h2>Kiosk tablets</h2>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            On a wall-mounted kiosk tablet, keyboard shortcuts are <strong>off by default</strong> and
            none of the keys below do anything. A kiosk is a shared, unattended device, so a keyboard
            plugged into it could otherwise drive the editing shortcuts &mdash; including
            <kbd>d</kbd>, which deletes a hold.
        </p>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            A wall admin can turn them on for one wall at a time, from their own device, under that
            wall's <strong>Kiosk Registration</strong> settings.
        </p>
    </section>

    @* One <dl> per surface, one <div class="keyshort-row"> per binding: adding or editing a
       binding later means touching exactly one markup block. *@

    <section class="keyshort-section">
        <h2>Global</h2>
        <p class="keyshort-note">Handled in JavaScript, so it works on every page, including pages with no interactivity.</p>
        <dl class="keyshort-list">
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>?</kbd></dt>
                <dd>
                    Open this keyboard shortcuts page <strong>in a new tab</strong>, so looking a key up
                    never leaves the page you are on or throws away staged edits
                </dd>
            </div>
        </dl>
    </section>

    <section class="keyshort-section">
        <h2>Wall page</h2>
        <p class="keyshort-note">Paging through the wall carousel. Disabled while the carousel is locked for editing.</p>
        <dl class="keyshort-list">
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>&larr;</kbd></dt>
                <dd>Previous pane</dd>
            </div>
            <div class="keyshort-row">
                <dt><kbd>&rarr;</kbd></dt>
                <dd>Next pane</dd>
            </div>
        </dl>
        <p class="keyshort-note">
            The arrows also stand down whenever a review stepper, an image lightbox or a modal dialog is
            open. In the gallery lightbox that means <kbd>&larr;</kbd>/<kbd>&rarr;</kbd> page through the
            images (and <kbd>Esc</kbd> closes it) instead of moving the carousel behind it.
        </p>
    </section>

    <KeyboardShortcutsEditor />

    <KeyboardShortcutsWizard />
</div>
